Ansible: forks vs serial — What's the Difference?
While working with Ansible, understanding the difference between forks and serial can help you optimize performance and control rollout strategies. Let's break it down:

🔁 forks – Parallelism Setting (Global)
Controls how many hosts are processed in parallel by Ansible at once.
Set in ansible.cfg (forks = 5 by default).
Affects how many SSH connections Ansible opens at a time.

🔧 Example:
 If you have 50 hosts and forks=10, Ansible will process 10 hosts in parallel, then the next 10, and so on.

🔢 serial – Rolling Deployment (Per Play)
Defines how many hosts to process at a time within a playbook.
Great for rolling updates or zero-downtime deployments.
Overrides forks at the playbook level.

🔧 Example:
yaml
CopyEdit
- hosts: webservers
  serial: 2
Ansible will update 2 servers at a time — wait for them to complete, then move to the next 2.

📊 Key Differences
 Feature         forks (global)                       serial (per play)
 Scope           Affects all tasks globally       Affects a specific play
 Purpose       Controls parallelism              Controls rollout batches
 Where Set    ansible.cfg or CLI                 Inside playbook (serial)
 Use Case      Speed/performance            Rolling updates, safe deploys

🧠 Pro Tip:
Use forks to boost speed, use serial to ensure stability.
